DOM 화면 분할 패널. (원본 web/pane.js 의 PaneStyl:
DOM screen split pane. (the web version porting PaneStyle and Pane of the original web/pane.js to the DOM as is, common types and calculations in web/pane.rs)
  ```text
    let main_pane = Pane::new(PaneOptions { direction: PaneDirection::Horizontal, ..Default::default() });
    let left_pane = Pane::new(PaneOptions { size: PaneSize::Fixed(240.0), min_size: 170.0, ..Default::default() });
    let view_pane = Pane::new(PaneOptions { min_size: 200.0, ..Default::default() });
    main_pane.add_pane(&left_pane);
    main_pane.add_pane(&view_pane);
    left_pane.get_container().append_child(&PaneStyle::create("div", "panel", &PaneStyleOptions::default()));
    main_pane.attach_to(&body);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  - PaneStyle::apply styles are a list of (CSS property name, value). Names are CSS names (background-color), not the original object keys (backgroundColor).
    (Rust has no Object.assign, so they are set with style.setProperty)
    If the tag name is invalid, it stops on the spot as in the original. (an exception in the original, a panic in the Rust version)
  - Pane is a handle that shares its state as `Rc<RefCell>`. clone points to the same pane. Children point to their parent with Weak.
    The window resize listener added by attach_to holds the root pane until the end. (same as the original) Panes that were not attach_to'd are freed when all handles are dropped, and dragging their resizers does nothing.
  - The resizer thickness is written to the data-thickness attribute as in the original, but calculations use the value held on the Rust side.
  - Listeners are wasm-bindgen Closures. Those attached to elements are handed to the JS garbage collector with into_js_value, and the element is read from the event's currentTarget.
    (holding the element would make the Rust side keep it alive, so it would not be freed even when removed from the DOM)
  - set_callback can be cleared with None. Calling into the pane again inside the callback is fine. (it is taken out while being called and put back afterward)

UI 씬. (원본 ui/uiscene.js 의 UIScene)\n- Ga:
UI scene. (UIScene of the original ui/uiscene.js)
- A scene with the same common frame as GameScene (developer tools, touch routing, loading screen, safe area insets, view size change detection, canvas background fill).
- Holds its own LayoutSolver, and every tick attaches the solver to the UINodes in the tree (attach_pending_in_tree), solves once, then moves the values onto the nodes.
- Screen guide UINode (screen_node): top left fixed at (0, 0), width and height are edit variables corrected with suggest_value when the size changes. (UIKit's UIWindow.bounds)
- Safe area guide UINode (safe_area_layout_guide): inset from the screen guide by the safe area insets. It adjusts the insets by itself at layout.
  (UIKit's UIView.safeAreaLayoutGuide)
- In the Rust version, instead of inheritance, the scene holds a UISceneBase, implements the UIScene trait, then gets a Scene with one line of ui_scene!. (same form as GameScene, game_scene!)
  ```text
    struct MenuScene { ui_scene_base: UISceneBase }
    impl UIScene for MenuScene {
        fn get_ui_scene_base(&self) -> &UISceneBase { &self.ui_scene_base }
        fn get_ui_scene_base_mut(&mut self) -> &mut UISceneBase { &mut self.ui_scene_base }
        fn initialize(&mut self, engine: &mut Engine) { /* create nodes with UINode attached and add constraints with the get_safe_area_layout_guide anchors */ }
    }
    vanilla::ui_scene!(MenuScene);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  - The original inherits Scene directly and carries the same code as GameScene separately. The Rust version holds the common frame as a GameSceneBase (without creating the safe area node)
    and adds only the solver and the two guides. So GameSceneBase's screen fade and play input can also be used. (not in the original UIScene)
  - The original creates the solver and guides in create. The Rust version does not need the engine, so it creates them at construction. The "apply the view size to the solver right away" at the end of the original load is done in initialize.
  - The safe area insets are LayoutMargins instead of a { top, left, bottom, right } object. Solver errors are returned as `Result<(), LayoutError>`.

스프라이트 애니메이터 컴포넌트.\n- 원본 misc/spriteanima:
Sprite animator component.
- A port of the original misc/spriteanimator.js. Register named clips (frame sequences) and switch with play("run").
- Playing the same clip again does not restart it. (can be changed with restart_if_same)
- When a clip ends, the on_complete callback continues to the next state. (used together with an FSM)
- Puts the current frame (image + area) into the Sprite component of the same node. In tick it is found with context.get_node_mut().`get_component_mut::<Sprite>()`.
- Usage:
  ```text
    let animator = node_tree[hero_id].add_component(SpriteAnimator::new());
    animator.add_clip("idle", image.clone(), &idleRects, 10.0, true);
    animator.add_clip("attack", image.clone(), &attackRects, 12.0, false);
    SpriteAnimator::play_on_node(&mut node_tree[hero_id], "attack", SpriteAnimatorPlayOptions::create_with_on_complete(|animator| {
        animator.play("idle", SpriteAnimatorPlayOptions::default());
    }));
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  play cannot put the frame into the Sprite on the spot as in the original. Rust components do not point to their own node (NodeTree holds the nodes,
    and only tick's ComponentContext hands the node to a component), and while the animator is borrowed &mut from the node, the same node's Sprite cannot be borrowed too.
    So it is put in on the next tick. To put it in on the spot, use play_on_node, which takes the node.
  on_complete is a one-shot callback that receives the animator (FnOnce(&mut SpriteAnimator)). In the original, the closure captured the animator.
  fps is not abbreviated, so it is frame_per_second. The defaults (10, looping) are passed as DEFAULT_FRAME_PER_SECOND and true.
  Atlas JSON is taken as serde_json::Value. The original findSprite (find and hold on to it) was not ported because a reference to a sibling component cannot be held (a borrow cannot live past tick).
    Instead, each tick finds it with context.get_node_mut().`get_component_mut::<Sprite>()`, and from outside, pass a Sprite to apply_current_frame(sprite).

제약 솔버. (원본 ui/autolayout/layoutsolver.js:
Constraint solver. (LayoutSolver of the original ui/autolayout/layoutsolver.js, based on Kiwi dual simplex)
- Usage:
  let solver = LayoutSolver::new();
  let x = LayoutVariable::new("x");
  let y = LayoutVariable::new("y");
  solver.add_constraint(&LayoutExpression::from_variable(&x).equal_to(10))?;
  solver.add_constraint(&LayoutExpression::from_variable(&y).equal_to(LayoutExpression::from_variable(&x).add(20)))?;
  solver.update_variables();
  x.get_value(); // 10
  y.get_value(); // 30
- Dynamic input (drag, etc):
  solver.add_edit_variable(&x, LayoutStrength::STRONG)?;
  solver.suggest_value(&x, 50.0)?;
  solver.update_variables();
- Differences from the original:
  - In the original, several UINodes and UIScenes share one object. The Rust version is a cheap Clone handle holding an `Rc<RefCell>`, and all functions take &self.
  - Where the original throws exceptions (duplicate constraints, unsatisfiable required constraints, unknown constraints and edit variables, required edit strength,
    internal solver failures), it returns `Result<(), LayoutError>`. The messages are the same as the original, except the long dashes were changed to colons.
  - Among the original's Maps, the row map, whose order changes which symbol is chosen, is a LayoutSymbolMap that keeps insertion order.
    The constraint, variable and edit variable maps, which do not depend on order, are HashMaps.
  - The null symbol of the original LayoutTag is the invalid symbol (LayoutSymbol::invalid). (same as Kiwi)

창 층. (vanilla.js 의 ui/uiwindowlayer.js,:
Window layer. (vanilla.js ui/uiwindowlayer.js, UIWindowLayer)
- Stacks UIWindows over the area. Opening a window stacks it on top, and only the top window is visible. When the upper window closes, the window below becomes visible again.
- On close it leaves the list right away so presses and covering are released immediately, and after the close motion (PopupMotion) finishes the window node is detached from the layer. Closing a covered window detaches it right away.
  The detached window node is not removed, so it can be opened again with open_window. (same as the original) The scene removes windows it will not reuse with destroy_node.
- The top window gets the wheel first (UIWindowBehavior::handle_wheel), and if it does not take it, the scroll view at that spot does. (scroll_window_at, GameSceneBase passes wheel input pointing inside the window layer)
- This layer is enlarged by the UI scale (UIScale::read), and windows are told the area size and safe area widths divided by that much. (so text is readable even on small screens)
  With set_ui_scale_options(None), it does not enlarge.
- While even one window is open, play is suspended. It notifies when windows open and close (set_window_change_event, passes the number of open windows). The scene may check has_open_window and pause play.
- Usage: (placed as the top child of the area node, notified whenever the area size changes)
  ```text
    let layer_id = UIWindowLayer::create(node_tree, play_area_id);
    node_tree.with_component_mut::<UIWindowLayer, _>(layer_id, |layer, tree| {
        layer.set_view_size(tree, width, height, view_manager.get_css_pixel_scale());
        layer.set_view_bleed(tree, top, right, bottom, left);
    });
    let window_id = UIConfirmWindow::create(node_tree, layer_id, "quit", UIConfirmWindowOptions { title: ..., ..UIConfirmWindowOptions::default() });
    UIWindowLayer::open_window_in_tree(node_tree, layer_id, window_id);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  - In the original it is a node inheriting WorldNode. The Rust version is a component attached to the layer node, and windows are child nodes of that node. Functions that change things take the tree.
  - The original reopens a detached window object if it is held. In the Rust version the detached node remains in the tree storage, so it is reopened with that handle.
    In the original a window held by no one is cleaned up by the garbage collector, but in the Rust version the scene removes it or it is removed together when the scene ends.
  - In the original the layer asks UIScale for the engine's CSS scale. In the Rust version set_view_size takes the CSS scale (ViewManager::get_css_pixel_scale).
  - The window notification is `Box<dyn FnMut(usize)>`. (number of open windows)

타일맵. (초안: 0.4.0 뒤 기능, API 가 바뀔 수 있다, 원본:
Tilemap. (Draft: a feature after 0.4.0, the API may change, the original src/experimental/tilemap/)
- Tileset: a grid of one image (margin, spacing) or an image per tile, with per-tile properties, type, animation and collision shapes.
- TilemapLayer: cell storage split into chunks (chunk), color, opacity, offset, blend mode. Holds endless maps (negative cells) too.
- Tilemap: a component attached to a node. Coordinate conversion and drawing for orthogonal, isometric, staggered and hexagonal orientations (off-screen culling per chunk, batching tiles with the same image),
  the tile animation clock, flip and rotation bits (TileFlag), and cell collision shapes.
- RuleTile: an auto tile that picks tiles by rules on the 8 surrounding cells. (same rules and transforms as Unity RuleTile)
- TiledMapReader: reads Tiled JSON maps (.tmj) and tilesets (.tsj).

```text
let mut tilemap = Tilemap::new();
TiledMapReader::read_map(&map_json, &mut tilemap, "orthogonal.tmj", Some(&read_tileset))?;
TiledMapReader::attach_images(&mut tilemap, &read_image);
node.add_component(tilemap);
```

타자기 연출기.\n- 원본 misc/typewriter.js 를 옮긴 것:
Typewriter effect.
- A port of the original misc/typewriter.js. Reveals the text of a target that can set its visible character count, like Text, one character at a time at a set speed per second.
  (visual novel / RPG dialogue presentation)
- Usage:
  ```text
    let mut typewriter = Typewriter::new(TypewriterOptions { characters_per_second: 30.0, ..TypewriterOptions::default() });
    let label = node_tree[label_id].get_component_mut::<Text>().unwrap();
    typewriter.start(label, Some("Hello. The adventure begins."));
    // every frame: typewriter.tick(label, time_delta);
    // on input: next line if finished (typewriter.is_finished()), otherwise typewriter.skip_to_end(label)
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  The original takes the target in the constructor and holds it. The Rust version cannot hold a component inside a node, so
  the target (&mut dyn TypewriterTarget) is passed on every start / tick / skip_to_end. Text implements TypewriterTarget.
  As in the original, characters are counted in grapheme clusters (final consonants, combining marks, and emoji sequences count as one). The character reveal notification receives (grapheme cluster, index).
  The options object is the TypewriterOptions struct.

터치 효과 컴포넌트.\n- 원본 misc/toucheffect.js 를:
Touch effect component.
- A port of the original misc/toucheffect.js. Scatters sky blue light particles at the press point that fade and spread over 0.4 seconds.
- Particles draw a white radial gradient image (64x64, made only once) tinted sky blue (rgb 100, 200, 255) with additive (lighter) blending.
- Usage:
  ```text
    let touch_effect_id = node_tree.create_child(root_id, "TouchEffect");
    node_tree[touch_effect_id].set_pivot(Pivot::TOP_LEFT);
    node_tree[touch_effect_id].add_component(TouchEffect::new());
    // on press: node_tree[touch_effect_id].get_component_mut::<TouchEffect>().unwrap().create_touch_particle(x, y);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  The original is a node that inherits WorldNode, and the Rust version is a component attached to a node. As in the original, the blend is switched to additive in push_transform
  and restored to the previous blend in pop_transform, so children are drawn additively too. (the component's hooks around the node draw state)
  The gradient is an Image built directly from pixels instead of an offscreen canvas.

트레일 렌더러 컴포넌트.\n- 원본 effect/trailrenderer:
Trail renderer component.
- A port of the original effect/trailrenderer.js. Records the places a node passed as points and draws them as a band whose width / color fades out. (the 2D counterpart of Unity's TrailRenderer)
- Points are recorded in the node's local position (parent coordinates), and when drawing they are converted to coordinates relative to the node origin and drawn under the node transform. (same as the original)
- One draw_line per segment, so batching gathers them into one draw call. (with additive blending it breaks before and after the blend change)
- Usage:
  ```text
    let trail = node.add_component(TrailRenderer::new());
    trail.set_time(0.5);
    trail.set_width(14.0, 0.0);
    trail.set_colors(Color::create(0.4, 0.8, 1.0, 0.9), Color::create(0.4, 0.8, 1.0, 0.0));
  ```
- Differences from the original: the original passes colors as "rgba(r, g, b, a)" strings, so RGB is rounded to 0 ~ 255 integers. The Rust version does the same rounding and passes a Color.

Platformer physics component.
- A port of the original misc/platformerbody.js. Holds the minimal physics (kinematics) that side-scrolling games kept rebuilding,
  like gravity integration, ground contact, and horizontal movement bounds. Moves the node's local position directly. (down is +Y)
- Usage:
  ```text
    let body = node_tree[hero_id].add_component(PlatformerBody::new());
    body.set_ground_y(560.0);
    body.set_horizontal_bounds(Some(40.0), Some(920.0));
    // move: body.set_velocity_x(±speed), jump: body.jump(-720.0)
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  null bounds are None. The landing notification is an FnMut() callback.

화면 효과. (씬 후처리 사슬)\n- 원본 effect/screeneff:
Screen effect. (scene post-processing chain)
- Ported from the original effect/screeneffect.js. Effect names, apply order, default parameters, parameter names, default colors and GLSL bodies are copied from the original tables as they are.
- The scene is drawn to the screen as is, and end() copies the screen (or the area set with set_region), applies the enabled effects in order and puts it back in the same place.
  The screen is copied into an RGB8 texture with copyTexSubImage2D, and effects are applied with FullscreenPass going back and forth between ping-pong / half size framebuffers. The last step draws to the default framebuffer.
- The area is in canvas pixel coordinates (top left origin), and with a rounded corner radius the outside of it keeps the original.
- Shaders are GLSL ES 3.00. On desktop (GL 3.3 core) ShaderProgram changes only the header to #version 330 core, and the web (WebGL2) uses them as they are.
- The engine is not modified. Like the original, the scene calls begin at the start of pre_draw, end at the end of post_draw, and tick in tick:
  ```text
    // in the scene create
    self.screen_effect = ScreenEffect::new();
    self.screen_effect.set_enabled(ScreenEffectType::Vignette, true);
    // at the start of pre_draw / at the end of post_draw
    self.screen_effect.begin(graphic);  ...  self.screen_effect.end(graphic);
    // in tick
    self.screen_effect.tick(time_delta);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  - The original takes a Graphic in the constructor and holds the GL context. The Rust version is made taking nothing, makes GL resources at the first end, and each function takes a Graphic.
    At that time it holds a weak handle to the GL context, so destroy() releases without arguments like the original, and it also releases by itself when dropped (Drop, when the scene is unloaded).
  - Effects are the ScreenEffectType enum instead of strings. (the original string names are get_name / from_name)
  - When building a pass shader fails, the original throws an exception, and the Rust version reports once and skips that step.

Screen effect. (scene post-processing chain)
- A port of the original effect/screeneffect.js. Effect names, application order, default parameters, parameter names, default colors and GLSL bodies are carried over from the original table as they are.
- The scene draws straight to the screen, and in end() the screen (or the area set with set_region) is copied, the enabled effects are applied in order, and it is put back in the same place.
  The screen is copied to an RGB8 texture with copyTexSubImage2D, and effects are applied with FullscreenPass, going back and forth between ping-pong / half size framebuffers. The last step draws to the default framebuffer.
- The area is in canvas pixel coordinates (top left origin), and if a rounded corner radius is given, the original is kept outside it.
- Shaders are GLSL ES 3.00. On desktop (GL 3.3 core), ShaderProgram changes only the header to #version 330 core, and the web (WebGL2) uses them as they are.
- The engine is not modified. Like the original, the scene calls begin at the start of pre_draw, end at the end of post_draw, and tick in tick:
  ```text
    // in the scene's create
    self.screen_effect = ScreenEffect::new();
    self.screen_effect.set_enabled(ScreenEffectType::Vignette, true);
    // at the start of pre_draw / the end of post_draw
    self.screen_effect.begin(graphic);  ...  self.screen_effect.end(graphic);
    // in tick
    self.screen_effect.tick(time_delta);
  ```
- Differences from the original:
  - The original takes Graphic in the constructor and holds the GL context. The Rust version is created taking nothing, GL resources are made at the first end, and each function takes Graphic.
    So destroy also takes Graphic. If destroy is not called before taking down the scene, GL resources stay until the context goes away.
  - Effects are the ScreenEffectType enum instead of strings. (the original string names via get_name / from_name)
  - If creating a pass shader fails, the original throws an exception, and the Rust version reports it once and skips that step.

